issues.problematic_code_snippet:
// Ensure we're authenticated before proceeding with sign-out
await Task.Yield();

var state = await AuthenticationProvider.GetAuthenticationStateAsync();

private async Task ProcessLogOut(string returnUrl)
{
    if (!ValidateSignOutRequestState())
    {
        Log.LogoutOperationInitiatedExternally(Logger);
        Navigation.NavigateTo(ApplicationPaths.LogOutFailedPath, AuthenticationNavigationOptions with { HistoryEntryState = "The logout was not initiated from within the page." });
        return;
    }

public void Redirect()
{
    if (_tokenResult.InteractionOptions != null)
    {
        _navigation.NavigateToLogin(_tokenResult.InteractiveRequestUrl, _tokenResult.InteractionOptions);
    }
    else
    {
        _navigation.NavigateTo(_tokenResult.InteractiveRequestUrl!);
    }

findings.body:
The diff adds `await Task.Yield();` between `AuthenticationState.ReturnUrl = returnUrl;` and `GetAuthenticationStateAsync()`, with the comment "Ensure we're authenticated before proceeding with sign-out". Yielding does not check authentication. The `isauthenticated` check that follows already does that. The yield also changes scheduling for the sign-out path and is not mentioned in the PR description, which lists the auth changes in detail.

If the yield is there to work around a render or dispatcher ordering issue, the comment should say so and a test should cover it. If it is a leftover, it should be removed.

::diff{file="src/Components/WebAssembly/WebAssembly.Authentication/src/RemoteAuthenticatorViewCore.cs" lines="288-292"}

::callout{type="warning"}
No test in the diff exercises this line. The comment describes behavior that the code does not implement.

The `ProcessLogOut` condition changed from `(state != null && !valid) || (state == null && !await SignOutManager.ValidateSignOutState())` to `!ValidateSignOutRequestState()`. A null `HistoryEntryState` now fails validation unconditionally. That follows from deleting `SignOutSessionStateManager`, so the logic is probably intended. The PR description, however, says the change "only fail[s] validation when state exists but is invalid", which is the reverse.

The test removed in this PR, `AuthenticationManager_Logout_RedirectsToFailureOnInvalidSignOutState`, was the only test that asserted the failure redirect to `logout-failed` with the message "The logout was not initiated from within the page." No new test covers the null-state rejection. Reviewers relying on the description may assume the null path is unchanged.

::diff{file="src/Components/WebAssembly/WebAssembly.Authentication/src/RemoteAuthenticatorViewCore.cs" lines="280-287"}

::callout{type="info"}
If the new behavior is intended, the fix is to correct the description and restore a test for the failure redirect using a null history state. The test can drop the `SignOutManager` setup.

Before this PR, `Redirect()` took the `NavigateToLogin` branch only when both `InteractionOptions` and `InteractiveRequestUrl` were non-null. The PR narrows the check to `InteractionOptions != null`. `InteractiveRequestUrl` is declared `string?`, and `NavigateToLogin` takes a non-nullable `string loginPath`, so this call should now produce a nullable warning (CS8604). If the build treats warnings as errors, it will fail. At runtime, an `AccessTokenResult` built with non-null options and a null URL reaches `NavigateToLogin(null, ...)`, which dereferences the path. The public constructor accepts this combination. The in-repo framework path always sets both values together, so this is mainly a risk for custom `IAccessTokenProvider` implementations.

::diff{file="src/Components/WebAssembly/WebAssembly.Authentication/src/Services/AccessTokenNotAvailableException.cs" lines="38-45"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The else branch change itself is a good fix. It removes the dependency on `RedirectUrl`. The problem is only the dropped guard in the first branch.

The PR description says "Removed obsolete `ResourceAsset` constructor overload", and `PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt` records `*REMOVED*` for `ResourceAsset(string! url, IReadOnlyList<ResourceAssetProperty!>? properties)`. The diff never touches `src/Components/Components/src/ResourceAsset.cs`, and the base and head versions are identical. At head the class has one constructor, `ResourceAsset(string url, IReadOnlyList<ResourceAssetProperty>? properties = null)`, which matches the shipped signature. The removed entry describes a signature without the default value, which doesn't exist in the code.

Either the source change was left out of this diff, or the entry is stale. In the stale case it should be deleted, and the description should be corrected. In the missing-change case, the PR is incomplete and the constructor is still public.

::diff{file="src/Components/Components/src/PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt" lines="1-4"}

This PR removes `InternalCalls.InvokeJS<T0,T1,T2,TRes>`, which is bound through the Mono runtime. The removed comment says the namespace, type, and method name must match entries in Mono's `driver.c`. The PR description does not mention this removal. The class is internal, so the public API is unaffected. The risk is runtime-side: if an older or shipped WebAssembly runtime still binds this InternalCall, removing it can cause a missing-method failure at startup or on the first JS call.

I did not find any managed caller in the tree, so the managed build is safe. Whether the native side still needs it is outside this diff.

